.TH "STLINK" "3" "2026\-09\-23" "libstlink" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
stlink \- talk to an ST\-LINK programmer, and through it to an STM32
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<stlink/stlink.h>\f[R]
#include \f[B]<stlink/device.h>\f[R]
#include \f[B]<stlink/flash.h>\f[R]

void stlink::init();
void stlink::init(const std::string &chips_dir);

std::vector<stlink::BasicDeviceInfo> stlink::Discovery::enumerate();
std::vector<stlink::DeviceInfo>      stlink::Discovery::find(const DeviceFilter &filter);

stlink::Result<std::unique_ptr<stlink::IDevice>> stlink::IDevice::create(const DeviceInfo &info);
stlink::Result<std::unique_ptr<stlink::IDevice>> stlink::IDevice::create(const BasicDeviceInfo &info);
.EE
.PP
Link with \f[I]\-lstlink\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]libstlink\f[R] connects to an ST\-LINK programmer of any generation
and reaches the STM32 behind it.
A caller works with devices and chips; probes, opcodes and USB endpoints
are the library\(cqs own business and are not exposed.
.PP
Everything is C++17 in namespace \f[B]stlink\f[R].
A C binding is planned and does not exist yet.
.SS Starting the library
\f[B]stlink::init\f[R]() reads the chip descriptions the library was
installed with.
Nothing else works until it has been called, and calling it twice does
nothing.
It looks in \f[I]$STLINK_CHIPS_DIR\f[R], then in
\f[I]../share/stlink/config/chips\f[R] relative to the running program,
then in \f[I]./chips\f[R] beside it, and uses the first that exists.
.PP
\f[B]stlink::init\f[R](\f[I]chips_dir\f[R]) reads that directory and
nothing else, which is how a caller supplies their own descriptions.
A file that cannot be read or does not parse is logged and skipped, so
one bad file does not stop the library starting.
.SS Finding what is attached
\f[B]Discovery::enumerate\f[R]() lists every attached programmer without
touching any target, so it is safe to call while other targets are
running.
It returns a \f[I]BasicDeviceInfo\f[R] per probe, carrying the
generation, serial, USB identifiers and where it is attached.
.PP
\f[B]Discovery::find\f[R]() goes further and connects through to the
chip behind each probe, which is what lets it filter on what the chip
is.
It returns a \f[I]DeviceInfo\f[R], which is a \f[I]BasicDeviceInfo\f[R]
together with a \f[I]ChipInfo\f[R].
An empty result means nothing matched, which is not an error.
.PP
A \f[I]DeviceFilter\f[R] may constrain the serial, the generation, or
the chip identifier; every field is optional and
\f[B]DeviceFilter::NoFilter\f[R] accepts everything.
The serial and generation are checked before anything is opened, so
filtering on those costs no traffic.
.SS Opening a device
\f[B]IDevice::create\f[R]() connects and returns a device.
One device exists per programmer: a second \f[B]create\f[R]() for a
programmer that is already open fails with \f[B]ErrorCode::Busy\f[R]
rather than returning the first.
The device is connected when it is created and disconnected when it is
destroyed, and it leaves debug mode on the way out, so a target that was
running when it was found is running again afterwards.
.PP
Given a \f[I]BasicDeviceInfo\f[R] the chip is read on connection, since
a basic sheet does not say what it is.
.SS Working with the target
A device can \f[B]halt\f[R](), \f[B]run\f[R](), \f[B]step\f[R]() and
\f[B]reset\f[R]() the core, and report whether it is running with
\f[B]is_running\f[R]().
.PP
\f[B]read\f[R]() and \f[B]write\f[R]() move target memory.
\f[B]read_register\f[R]() and \f[B]write_register\f[R]() reach the core
registers by index.
.PP
\f[B]info\f[R]() returns the \f[I]DeviceInfo\f[R] the device was opened
with, including the chip\(cqs memory map: flash, SRAM, the system
bootloader, option bytes and OTP, each with a base and a size.
A size of zero means the chip does not have that region.
The flash size is read from the chip rather than taken from a file,
because one part number ships in several sizes.
.SS Flash
\f[B]flash\f[R]() returns the chip\(cqs flash controller, owned by the
device and valid for as long as it is.
.PP
In this release no flash family is implemented and every operation
returns \f[B]ErrorCode::NotSupported\f[R].
The interface is stable; the implementations are not yet written.
.PP
Nothing in that interface is implicit.
Erasing does not happen because a write needed it, and nothing is
verified unless verification was asked for.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Operations that can fail return \f[I]VoidResult\f[R], or
\f[I]Result\f[R] if they produce a value.
Ask \f[B]ok\f[R]() first.
\f[B]error\f[R]() describes the failure and \f[B]value\f[R]() takes the
value, and each throws \f[B]BadResultAccess\f[R] if asked when it does
not apply, because asking for a value that is not there is a mistake in
the caller rather than something the device did.
.PP
\f[I]Result\f[R] is move only, and \f[B]value\f[R]() moves the value
out, so it may be taken only once.
.SH ERRORS
An \f[I]Error\f[R] carries an \f[I]ErrorCode\f[R], a context saying what
was being attempted, and optionally the error that caused it.
\f[B]describe\f[R]() renders the whole chain, outermost first, separated
by \(rq <\- \(lq:
.IP
.EX
erasing the sector <\- writing flash <\- the probe refused the command
.EE
.PP
No foreign error code ever reaches a caller.
A libusb or Win32 failure is translated, so the vocabulary in
\f[I]stlink/error.h\f[R] is the whole vocabulary.
.PP
The codes are \f[B]IO\f[R], \f[B]NotFound\f[R], \f[B]AccessDenied\f[R],
\f[B]Busy\f[R], \f[B]Disconnected\f[R], \f[B]Timeout\f[R],
\f[B]Protocol\f[R], \f[B]TargetUnknown\f[R], \f[B]TargetRefused\f[R],
\f[B]NotSupported\f[R], \f[B]InvalidArgument\f[R], \f[B]OutOfRange\f[R],
\f[B]OutOfMemory\f[R] and \f[B]Internal\f[R].
\f[B]Internal\f[R] means a broken invariant in the library itself and is
worth reporting as a bug.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
One device belongs to one thread at a time and nothing is internally
synchronised.
Two threads may drive two devices; they may not drive one.
.SH LOGGING
Logging is not the error channel: a failure reaches the caller as a
return value, and logging is the detail behind it.
\f[B]Log::set_sink\f[R]() installs a sink that receives every record,
and an empty sink silences the library.
\f[B]Log::set_threshold\f[R]() discards anything less severe than the
kind given; the default is \f[B]LogKind::Info\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
stlink::init();

\f[B]auto\f[R] found = stlink::Discovery::find({.chip_id = 0x450});

\f[B]if\f[R] (found.empty())
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] 1;
}

\f[B]auto\f[R] opened = stlink::IDevice::create(found.front());

\f[B]if\f[R] (!opened.ok())
{
    STLINK_LOG_ERR(\(dq%s\(dq, opened.error().describe().c_str());
    \f[B]return\f[R] 1;
}

\f[B]auto\f[R] device = opened.value();
\f[B]auto\f[R] halted = device\->halt();
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]$STLINK_CHIPS_DIR\f[R]
Chip descriptions, if set, in preference to the installed ones.
.TP
\f[I]../share/stlink/config/chips\f[R]
Where the installed chip descriptions are, relative to the program.
